# Playwright Conventions

## Refusals (hard stops)

Refuse each of these. Stop and raise the conflict. Do not comply.

- **Weakening or deleting an assertion to pass.** Refuse. Stop and raise the conflict.
- **Healing a failure triage classified as a real bug.** Refuse. Stop and raise the conflict.
- **Replacing a role-based locator with CSS or XPath.** Refuse. Stop and raise the conflict.
- **`waitForTimeout`.** Refuse. Stop and raise the conflict.
- **A hardcoded credential.** Refuse. Stop and raise the conflict.
- **The `any` type.** Refuse. Stop and raise the conflict.

## 1. Locators

Role, then label or placeholder, then text; `getByTestId` only with a comment. Never CSS or XPath.

✅ Good (`pages/LoginPage.ts`)

```ts
this.signInButton = page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign In' });
this.emailField = page.getByLabel('Email');
```

❌ Bad

```ts
this.signInButton = page.locator('button.btn-primary');
```

Why: A CSS or XPath locator fails when the markup shifts and the button is still the same control.

## 2. Waiting

Wait with web-first `expect(locator).toBeVisible()` / `.toBeEnabled()` / `.toHaveText()`. Never `waitForTimeout`.

✅ Good (`tests/programs.a11y.spec.ts`)

```ts
await expect(programs.programsHeading).toBeVisible();
```

❌ Bad (`tests/ds1-create-program.spec.ts`)

```ts
await programs.page.waitForTimeout(500);
```

Why: A fixed sleep passes or fails on the clock, so a slow response becomes a flake and a fast one wastes time.

## 3. Page Objects

One class per page or component in `pages/`. Locators are `readonly` in the constructor. Methods act and do not assert.

✅ Good (`pages/LoginPage.ts`)

```ts
constructor(page: Page) {
  this.page = page;
  this.signInButton = page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign In' });
}
async login(email: string, password: string): Promise<void> {
  await this.emailField.fill(email);
  await this.passwordField.fill(password);
  await this.signInButton.click();
}
```

❌ Bad

```ts
async login(email: string, password: string): Promise<void> {
  await this.emailField.fill(email);
  await expect(this.signInButton).toBeEnabled();
}
```

Why: An assertion inside a POM hides the failure in a helper, so the spec no longer states what the user should see.

## 4. Auth

Stored session from the setup project for every test except the login page. API login only for bulk setup, scripts, or a stale session.

✅ Good (`playwright.config.ts`, `tests/auth.setup.ts`)

```ts
storageState: 'playwright/.auth/user.json',
await page.context().storageState({ path: authFile });
```

❌ Bad

```ts
test.beforeEach(async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/login');
  await page.getByLabel('Email').fill(email);
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign In' }).click();
});
```

Why: A UI login in every test fails the suite when the login form is slow, and it couples feature tests to the login page.

## 5. Test data

Happy-path records come from Faker factories in `test-data/factories/`. Invalid values are curated lists in static `.ts` files under `test-data/`, not random strings. Secrets and base URLs come from `process.env`. Repeated paths and UI copy live in enums.

A factory still appends `Date.now()` so parallel workers do not collide. Every test that creates a program calls `trackProgram(uuid)`. The cleanup fixture deletes that row after the test. That is the revert for the test's own data, and the test keeps its importance or lane tag.

✅ Good (`test-data/factories/program.ts` — advised; no spec imports it yet)

```ts
import { faker } from '@faker-js/faker';

export type ProgramDraft = {
  name: string;
  description: string;
};

/** Happy-path program. The timestamp keeps the name unique under fullyParallel. */
export function buildProgram(overrides: Partial<ProgramDraft> = {}): ProgramDraft {
  return {
    name: `${faker.company.catchPhrase()} ${Date.now()}`,
    description: faker.lorem.sentence(),
    ...overrides,
  };
}
```

✅ Good (static invalid set, env, enum)

```ts
export const invalidProgramNames = ['', '   ', '\t', '\n'] as const;

const baseURL = process.env.DIDAXIS_URL;
const email = process.env.DIDAXIS_EMAIL;

export enum AppPath {
  Login = '/login',
  Programs = '/programs',
}

export enum DashboardCopy {
  Welcome = 'Welcome to Didaxis Studio',
  ManagePrograms = 'Manage academic programs',
}
```

```ts
const program = buildProgram();
trackProgram(await uuidFromProgramCreateResponse(await responsePromise));
```

❌ Bad

```ts
const name = 'Intro to Testing';
const email = 'qa@example.com';
await programs.createProgram(name);
```

Why: A shared name collides under `fullyParallel`, a literal account does not follow the environment, and an undeleted program piles up until later tests see the wrong row.

## 6. API testing

Use the `request` fixture for setup, teardown, and contract checks. Program deletes stay on `fixtures/cleanup.fixture.ts`.

✅ Good (advised — no spec takes the `request` fixture yet; `tests/manual/bulk-delete-programs.spec.ts` uses `page.request` and a `{ data: [...] }` body)

```ts
test('GET /api/programs returns a list', { tag: '@api' }, async ({ request }) => {
  const response = await request.get('/api/programs');
  expect(response.ok()).toBeTruthy();
  const body = await response.json();
  expect(Array.isArray(body.data)).toBe(true);
});
```

❌ Bad

```ts
test('list contract', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/programs');
  await expect(page.locator('table tr')).toHaveCount(3);
});
```

Why: A UI count checks the screen, so an API shape change slips through and the test depends on whoever left rows in the environment.

## 7. Network mocking

Fulfill `/api/programs` with `page.route` for 500, 503, timeout, an empty list, and a malformed body. Never mock the endpoint the test is verifying.

✅ Good (advised — no spec calls `page.route` yet)

```ts
await page.route('**/api/programs', async (route) => {
  if (route.request().method() !== 'POST') {
    await route.continue();
    return;
  }
  await route.fulfill({ status: 503, contentType: 'application/json', body: '{}' });
});
```

❌ Bad

```ts
await page.route('**/api/programs', (route) => route.fulfill({ status: 201, body: '{}' }));
await programs.newProgramModal.clickCreate();
expect(response.status()).toBe(201);
```

Why: Mocking the call under test makes a green result that never reached the server, so a real 500 never fails the spec.

## 8. Accessibility

Key pages get `new AxeBuilder({ page }).withTags(['wcag2a','wcag2aa']).analyze()` asserted with `expect(results.violations).toEqual([])`, plus a keyboard path. Scope with `.include()` / `.exclude()` only for a noisy third-party widget, and comment why. Never `.disableRules()`.

✅ Good (`tests/ds3-validate-program.spec.ts`)

```ts
const results = await new AxeBuilder({ page })
  .withTags(['wcag2a', 'wcag2aa'])
  .analyze();
await expect(results.violations).toEqual([]);
await expect(programs.newProgramModal.createButton).toBeFocused();
await page.keyboard.press('Enter');
```

❌ Bad (`tests/programs.a11y.spec.ts`)

```ts
.disableRules('button-name')
```

Why: `.disableRules()` turns a real violation into a pass, so the missing control name ships.

## 9. Assertions

Web-first expects. `expect.soft()` when one test checks several independent facts. A screenshot only where the layout difference is the thing under test.

✅ Good (`tests/ds3-validate-program.spec.ts`)

```ts
await expect(programs.newProgramModal.createButton).toBeEnabled();
```

❌ Bad

```ts
await expect(await programs.newProgramModal.createButton.isVisible()).toBe(true);
```

Why: A one-shot boolean does not retry, so the assertion fails while the button is still rendering.

## 10. Anti-flake

Retries only on CI, and never above 2. Do not set `workers: 1` on the default run. Pin `locale: 'en-CA'` and `timezoneId: 'America/Toronto'`. Isolate each test’s data. Freeze relative timestamps with `page.clock.install()`.

`npm test` and CI set `grepInvert: /@destructive/` so shared-state tests stay out of the parallel suite. `npm run test:destructive` is the exception: `--workers=1`, `fullyParallel: false`, and only the `@destructive` tag.

✅ Good (`playwright.config.ts`)

```ts
fullyParallel: true,
retries: process.env.CI ? 2 : 0,
grepInvert: /@destructive/,
locale: 'en-CA',
timezoneId: 'America/Toronto',
```

❌ Bad

```ts
retries: 3,
workers: 1,
```

Why: Extra retries hide a real bug, an unpinned timezone makes “2 hours ago” differ between a laptop and CI, and a permanent single worker hides races the rest of the suite still has.

## 11. Tags

Each `test()` carries exactly one strategy tag in the details object: `@smoke`, `@sanity`, `@regression`, `@api`, `@e2e`, or `@destructive`. Never on `test.describe()`.

| Tag | When |
|-----|------|
| `@smoke` | The critical happy path for an area. A small set. |
| `@sanity` | Another primary success path, or the main guard (empty required field, cancel). |
| `@regression` | Boundaries, reload edges, authz, unicode, whitespace, accessibility, and quarantined `test.fixme` cases. |
| `@api` | The assertion is the HTTP contract. No screen journey. |
| `@e2e` | A browser journey across product areas. |
| `@destructive` | The test writes shared state: locale, role, feature flag, or setting. |

`@destructive` is only that last case. A test that creates, edits, or deletes a program and `trackProgram` removes that row keeps `@smoke`, `@sanity`, `@regression`, `@api`, or `@e2e`.

Any test that changes persistent state restores it.

- Own records: `trackProgram` and the cleanup fixture, which runs after the test. Do not add a second `afterAll` for those rows.
- Locale, roles, flags, or settings: `test.afterEach` or `test.afterAll` writes the previous value back, including when the test fails. Tag `@destructive`. Call `test.describe.configure({ mode: 'serial' })`. Run that file only with `npm run test:destructive`.

A `test.fixme` title may still end in `@known-failure`. That marks a quarantine. The strategy tag is the `{ tag }` value.

✅ Good

```ts
test('admin creates a program', { tag: '@smoke' }, async ({ page }) => {});
```

```ts
// Advised — no spec writes locale, roles, flags, or settings yet.
test.describe.configure({ mode: 'serial' });

let previousLocale: string;

test.afterEach(async () => {
  await restoreLocale(previousLocale);
});

test('admin switches the locale', { tag: '@destructive' }, async () => {});
```

❌ Bad

```ts
test.describe('programs', { tag: '@regression' }, () => {});
test('creates a program', { tag: ['@smoke', '@e2e'] }, async () => {});
test('switches locale', { tag: '@smoke' }, async () => {});
```

Why: Two tags make `--grep` slices overlap. A describe tag stamps every test in the block. A locale left in place fails the next worker. `npm run test:smoke`, `test:sanity`, `test:regression`, `test:api`, `test:e2e`, and `test:destructive` each select one tag. The last script passes `--workers=1`.

## Do / Don't

| | Do | Don't |
|---|---|---|
| Locators | `getByRole`, then label, placeholder, text, test id | CSS, XPath, `.first()` |
| Waiting | `toBeVisible` / `toBeEnabled` / `toHaveText` | `waitForTimeout` |
| Page objects | One class; locators in the constructor | Assertions in `pages/` |
| Auth | `storageState` from the setup project | UI login in every test |
| Test data | Faker factory, `` `${prefix} ${Date.now()}` ``, `trackProgram`, `process.env`, enums | A fixed name, a hardcoded secret, or no delete |
| API | `request` fixture for setup and contracts | Prove the API through the UI |
| Mocking | `route.fulfill` for 500, 503, timeout, `[]`, malformed | Mock the call the test verifies |
| Accessibility | `wcag2a` / `wcag2aa` and a keyboard path | `.disableRules()` |
| Assertions | Web-first; `expect.soft` for multi-check | `expect(await locator.isVisible()).toBe(true)` |
| Anti-flake | CI retries ≤ 2; `en-CA`; `America/Toronto`; `page.clock`; `@destructive` only via `test:destructive` | `retries` above 2; `workers: 1` on the default run |
| Tags | One of `@smoke`, `@sanity`, `@regression`, `@api`, `@e2e`, `@destructive` on `test()` | A second tag, a tag on `describe`, `@destructive` for own-row cleanup |
